+*   **Compatibility**: The type of fragment processing in the Insertion stage must be strictly compatible with the Evaluation step to ensure correct interpretation of per-pixel information [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Accuracy vs. Performance:** Chosen over raytracing to circumvent problems arising from raytracing and self or global intersections, maintaining high accuracy for smoke volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Bottlenecks:** The Insertion step is identified as the greatest bottleneck of the entire rendering pipeline due to simultaneous access to shared indexing mechanisms [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware Dependency:** Relies heavily on GPU-related functionality, shader application, and memory transactions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Flexibility:** The pipeline allows switching between rendering methods during runtime without re-initializing the smoke volume, facilitating comparison of different approaches using identical configurations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Maintains high accuracy by avoiding raytracing artifacts (self-intersection, global intersection) [S1, unofficial]. Allows runtime switching between rendering methods without re-initializing the smoke volume [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: The Insertion stage is a significant performance bottleneck due to concurrent modification of shared memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Measured in the context of real-time smoke volume rendering (Smoker application) [S1, unofficial]. Specific hardware metrics are not provided in the sources.
 
 ## Background
-The Fragment List-Based Rendering Pipeline was implemented in the **Smoker** application for real-time smoke volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It was developed as part of a master's thesis by Dirk Sowinski in 2012 [S1, unofficial]. The approach generalizes the rendering process into a 4-stage classification to manage the complexity of GPU-based volume rendering and improve performance through specialized stages [S1, unofficial].
+The Fragment List-Based Rendering Pipeline was implemented in the **Smoker** application for real-time smoke volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It was developed as part of a master's thesis by **Sowinski** in **2012** [S1, unofficial]. The approach was chosen to provide a flexible framework that allows comparing different rendering strategies using identical configurations [S1, unofficial].
 
